target-measure-0e0d9273.symmetry_origin:
This formula is derived from kinetic theory and statistical mechanics. It is a direct consequence of considering the number of potential interaction events within a given volume and time interval. The rate is proportional to the number of available pairs of particles (
    $n_1 n_2$
    ) and the probability of a successful interaction for each pair, which is quantified by the cross-section (
    $\sigma$
    ) and their relative velocity (
    $v$
    ). The averaging (
    $\langle \dots \rangle$
    ) accounts for the velocity distribution of particles in a system.

schwarzschild-radius-ratio.conceptual_definition:
The formula represents the ratio of the Schwarzschild radius (
$r_s$
) to the total radius (
$r$
) of an object, and it approaches zero (
$r_s / r \to 0$
). This condition signifies that the object is not a black hole; its gravitational influence is not so strong as to trap light within its boundary.
